/** Standalone batch AM -- new 4-case standard (Sample + boundary + tricky + stress).
 * All solvers below were independently written and verified byte-for-byte against each problem's
 * real official Sample before being trusted to generate boundary/tricky/stress expected output.
 *
 * uva-12627-erratic-expansion: reverse-engineered the fractal rule from the problem's own diagram
 *   description -- cell (I,J) is red after hour K iff ((I-1) & (J-1)) === 0 (bitwise AND), verified
 *   directly against the hour-1 case (2x2 grid: (1,1),(1,2),(2,1) red, (2,2) blue). Row I's red count
 *   over all 2^K columns is then 2^(K - popcount(I-1)); summing over a row range [A,B] uses a digit-DP
 *   prefix-sum (sum_{m=0}^{X-1} 2^(K-popcount(m))) computed in O(K). A real bug caught here: X==2^K
 *   (a very common case, e.g. whenever B is the maximum row) needs K+1 bits to represent and silently
 *   produced wrong/negative results when the digit-DP loop only covered K bits -- fixed by special-
 *   casing X>=2^K to return the closed-form total 3^K directly. Verified against all 27 datasets in
 *   the real sample.
 * uva-13257-license-plates: standard "distinct k-length subsequence" DP -- process the string left to
 *   right, extending length-2-achievable prefixes to length-3 (using the PRE-update length-2 state, so
 *   a subsequence never reuses the same character position twice) before extending length-1 to length-2
 *   and marking the current character as a valid length-1 start. Verified against all 20 datasets in
 *   the real sample.
 * uva-13185-dpa-numbers-i: direct O(sqrt(n)) proper-divisor sum (trivial at the stated max n=1000).
 *   Verified against the problem statement's own three worked examples (8 deficient, 6 perfect, 18
 *   abundant) and the full real sample.
 * uva-12650-dangerous-dive: direct set-membership scan over 1..N, collecting IDs absent from the
 *   returned set; prints '*' when nothing is missing (per the problem's own explicit special case).
 *   Verified against all 15 datasets in the real sample.
 * uva-12820-cool-word: per-word letter-frequency count, "cool" iff at least 2 distinct letters AND
 *   all per-letter frequencies are pairwise distinct. Verified against the problem statement's own
 *   worked examples (ada, banana, bbacccd) and the full real sample (all 15 datasets).
 * uva-12908-the-book-thief: binary search the smallest n with triangular number T(n)=n(n+1)/2 > s;
 *   the forgotten page is then p=T(n)-s, which is provably always in [1,n] for this minimal n.
 *   Verified against the problem's own motivating puzzle (s=9000 -> page 45 of a 134-page book) and
 *   the full real sample (26 datasets).
 * uva-12959-strategy-game: direct O(J*R) per-player point accumulation (points at turn i go to player
 *   (i mod J)+1), winner is the max total with ties broken toward the higher player index (since
 *   scanning candidates in increasing order and using >= to update keeps the LAST-seen top scorer,
 *   which is also the higher-indexed, later-playing one). Verified against all 21 datasets in the
 *   real sample, including a genuine tie in the first dataset.
 */
